Any field of science, technology or application, as long as the research is high-risk, breakthrough and aimed at a radically new technology.
Mandatory (the four Gatekeepers, essential characteristics every proposal must meet):
- a convincing long-term vision of a radically new technology, with a commitment to reach an experimental proof of concept or lab validation;
- a concrete, novel and ambitious scientific breakthrough underpinning it, and a team able to realise it;
- a high-risk, high-gain approach in an interdisciplinary collaboration, with concrete objectives;
- an important global challenge or unsolved problem the technology could help solve.
Excluded:
- incremental or known approaches, and technology already validated in the lab that only seeks implementation funding;
- research directed towards nuclear energy applications, which belongs in Euratom calls;
- military applications: Pathfinder is for civil applications only.
Applicants should first check whether their project fits one of the 2027 Pathfinder Challenges (themed topics), which include advanced materials for electricity grids.

TenneT would be most credible as the problem owner that frames a hard grid problem and keeps the research aimed at a usable grid technology.
This is an interpretation. The call wants teams that include the actors needed to guide development and who could later take the technology forward. TenneT could:
- define a grid problem no current technology solves, such as losses, stability with power electronics, or monitoring of subsea assets;
- give operating requirements and data for the lab validation use cases;
- offer a test or simulation setting, for example the digital twin facility it runs with Delft University of Technology (NFDTES);
- advise on certification, standards and future adoption.
The research itself would sit with universities and research organisations.
